//! Go's `math/rand/v2` pseudo-random generators, with Go's legacy
//! `math/rand` in [`v1`].
//!
//! Seeded generators are bit-for-bit compatible with Go: for the same seed,
//! [`Pcg`], [`ChaCha8`] and every [`Rand`] method return exactly the values Go
//! returns on its 64-bit platforms. Go's `int`/`uint` map to `i64`/`u64` so
//! results do not depend on the Rust target's pointer width.
//!
//! One caveat comes from Go itself: rare `NormFloat64`/`ExpFloat64` tail values
//! differ in the last bit between Go on arm64 (which fuses multiply-add) and
//! Go on amd64. aarch64 builds reproduce Go's arm64 results; all other targets
//! reproduce Go's unfused amd64 results.
//!
//! ```
//! use rust_stdlib::math::rand::{Pcg, Rand};
//! let mut r = Rand::new(Pcg::new(1, 2));
//! assert_eq!(r.uint64(), 0xc4f5a58656eef510);
//! let roll = r.int_n(6) + 1;
//! assert!((1..=6).contains(&roll));
//! ```
//!
//! # Not for security
//!
//! None of this module is a cryptographically secure random number generator,
//! and it must not be used for keys, tokens or nonces; Go's `crypto/rand` is
//! pending (ADR 0003). The unseeded top-level functions such as [`int_n`] use
//! a per-thread [`ChaCha8`] seeded once per thread by [`GlobalSource`]: 32
//! bytes from `/dev/urandom` on Unix, mixed with std's randomly keyed
//! `RandomState` SipHash over the time, process id, thread id and a counter.
//! When `/dev/urandom` is unavailable (for example on Windows) only the mixed
//! `RandomState` material is used; std seeds those keys from the OS
//! generator. Unlike Go's runtime source, nothing here is reseeded after fork.

mod chacha8;
mod fmath;
mod pcg;
mod tables;
pub mod v1;
mod zipf;

